# The system monitor: a window that shows every second what each processor,
# the memory, the network and the threads are doing.
@cpus 4
@ai
$ ai control on
$ monitor
the system monitor in a new window: q leaves
$ sleep 2
@ctl text {machine}
~ ^--- window 1 ---$
~ ^ System monitor   up \d
~ ^ Processors: 4 in use$
~ ^  cpu 0  runs the kernel
~ ^  cpu 1  \[[#.]+\] +\d+% on jobs$
~ ^  cpu 3  \[[#.]+\] +\d+% on jobs$
~ ^  heap   \[[#.]+\] [\d.]+ [KM]iB of [\d.]+ MiB$
~ ^  in  [\d.]+ [KM]?i?B/s
~ ^ Threads: \d+$
~ ^  monitor +(running|ready|waiting)
~ q leave
# Work on all processors shows up as work on the others.
$ cpu test
~ ^same result; \d+\.\d times as fast$
@ctl keys {machine} "q"
$ sleep 1
$ threads
!~ monitor
$ monitor now
usage: monitor
